        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The Autonomic Neuroplasticity in Weightlessness experiment was one of four autonomic nervous system experiments performed during the 1998 Neurolab mission. This experiment concentrated on baroreceptors and the changes in cardiac-nervous system relationship. 
<p>
In various areas of the body, a complex system of pressure sensors sends signals to brain, which integrates all incoming information and causes resulting changes in the amount of blood pumping through the heart, or changes in blood vessels' resistance, thereby changing blood flow. A big question for Neurolab was: What effects does microgravity have on this finely tuned system? This can be determined by studying the baroreflex. A baroreflex is a reflex instigated by a baroreceptor, a collection of sensory nerve endings specialized to monitor changes in blood pressure. The main receptors lie in the carotid sinuses and the aortic arch, while others are found in the walls of other large arteries and veins and in the walls of the heart. Impulses from the receptors reach centers in the medulla; from here, autonomic activity is directed so that the heart rate and resistance of the peripheral blood vessels can be adjusted appropriately. Thus, the baroreflex acts to increase or decrease the blood pressure through constant monitoring and reflex action in order to maintain the necessary blood pressure in the body. 
<p>
Investigators evaluated baroreflex control of sympathetic and vasoconstriction mechanisms. With this objective, better understanding of the physiological condition in microgravity will be reached. This research holds promising potential of helping to define pathophysiological mechanisms that can then lead to therapeutic countermeasures for postural orthostatic tachycardia, acute myocardial infarction, and orthostatic hypotension. 
<p>
APPROACH: <br>
Baroreflex malfunction after weightlessness as well as autonomic responses to reduced blood volume were investigated with controlled frequency breathing, respiration, stroke volume, cardiac period, and Valsalva maneuvers. 
The Valsalva maneuver is an attempt to forcibly exhale with the nose and mouth closed. Each of four trials began with a 45 second baseline period, during which the subject breathed normally. Immediately prior to the Valsalva maneuver, the subject was instructed to take a normal inspiration and hold it and then exhale against a closed valve mouthpiece, driving the pressure up to the target value, which was 15 mmHg for two trials and 30 mmHg for another two trials. The subject maintained pressure for 15 seconds, whereupon the valve was opened so that he/she could breathe during the recovery period (60 seconds each). This maneuver test stimulates the pressure receptors in the neck and chest while measuring the responses. 
<p>
The controlled frequency breathing test allows for the natural oscillations in the control of blood pressure to be measured precisely. To start, the subject breathes normally to obtain a normal cuff blood pressure reading during the first minute. Subsequently, there was a six-minute period of random-rate breathing. This was followed by three 3-minute periods of fixed frequency breathing at 0.25, 0.10, and 0.05 Hz, respectively. Immediately following was 15 seconds of hyperventilation at 1 breath/second; which was immediately followed by a breath-hold as long as the subject was comfortable, typically two to three minutes. Following the breath-hold, the subject was monitored for an additional 30-second period. 
<p>
Pre- and post-flight cardiac output was measured using a standard method of rebreathing a gas mixture. At the onset of the procedures, subjects were instructed to take a slightly larger than normal inspiration of the gas mixture. A valve then closed, so that subjects were expiring into and inspiring from a bag. They then repeated this over several cycles of normal respiration. The concentrations of various gases were measured with a mass spectrometer. 
<p>
RESULTS: <br>
All three nominally scheduled session series were performed by the four payload crewmembers, one session early (Flight Day 2), the second session mid (Flight Days 7 and 8), and the third session late (Flight Days 12 and 13) in flight. However, the APT signal in the mid-mission session was lost, so the protocol was repeated for two subjects on Flight Day 10. Additional sessions were performed on Flight Days 2 and 8 by one subject on the early mission session. Data appear to be of good quality based on the initial evaluation. 
<p>
In this study, the hypothesis that space flight impairs cerebral autoregulation was studied in six astronauts approximately 72 and 23 days before, after 1 and 2 weeks in space (n = 4), on landing day, and 1 day after the 16-day Neurolab space shuttle mission. Beat-by-beat changes of photoplethysmographic mean arterial pressure and transcranial Doppler middle cerebral artery blood flow velocity were measured during 5 minutes of spontaneous breathing, 30 mmHg lower body suction to simulate standing in space, and 10 minutes of 60 degree passive upright tilt on Earth. Dynamic cerebral autoregulation was quantified by analysis of the transfer function between spontaneous changes of mean arterial pressure and cerebral artery blood flow velocity, in the very low- (0.02-0.07 Hz), low- (0.07-0.20 Hz) and high-frequency (0.20-0.35 Hz) ranges. Resting middle cerebral artery blood flow velocity did not change significantly from preflight values during or after space flight. Reductions of cerebral blood flow velocity during lower body suction were significant before space flight (P < 0.05, repeated measures ANOVA), but not during or after space flight. Absolute and percentage reductions of mean (+/- s.e.m.) cerebral blood flow velocity after 10 minutes upright tilt were smaller after than before spaceflight (absolute, -4 +/- 3 cm per sec after versus -14 +/- 3 cm per sec before, P = 0.001; and percentage, -8.0 +/- 4.8% after versus -24.8 +/- 4.4% before, P < 0.05), consistent with improved rather than impaired cerebral blood flow regulation. Low-frequency gain decreased significantly (P < 0.05) by 26, 23 and 27% after 1 and 2 weeks in space and on landing day, respectively, compared with preflight values, which is also consistent with improved autoregulation. It can be concluded that human cerebral autoregulation is preserved, and possibly even improved, by short-duration space flight.
<p>
